[Event "Sicilian Defence: the main line"]
[Result "0-1"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/0Jbuvv6d/eGTRINjD"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis"]

{ Today you learn the Sicilian from Black's side: a fighting answer to his king pawn, taking on the middle from the side with your c pawn. Against d4 you would need another opening. }
1. e4 { He opens with his king pawn. Meet it from the side: your c pawn goes two squares, to c5. [%cal Yc7c5] } 1... c5 { Your c pawn watches d4 from the side, so the middle is not his alone. [%cal Gc5d4] } (1... e5 { Very good too, but that is a different opening. The Sicilian meets his pawn from the side. } 2. Nf3 Nc6 { Level: a fine game, just another one. This study is about c5. [%csl Be5] }) (1... e6 { Solid, but another opening, where your pawn goes to d5 next. } 2. d4 d5 { About level: a sound game with pawns on e6 and d5, but not the Sicilian. [%csl Bd5] }) 2. Nf3 { His knight eyes d4. Against knight or pawn to c3, the plan is the same: e pawn one step, then d pawn two. Now push your e pawn. [%cal Ye7e6] } 2... e6 { The pawn on e6 guards d5 and opens a road for your dark bishop. [%cal Ge6d5,Gf8c5] } (2... d6 { Just as good: the most played Sicilian move, with a different set-up. } 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 { About level: another Sicilian, with your pawn on d6 instead of e6. [%csl Bd6] }) (2... Nc6 { Just as good: the knight first, another Sicilian set-up. } 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 { About level: a different Sicilian, with your knight already on c6. [%csl Bc6] }) 3. d4 { He opens the middle with d4. Against a slow set-up with d3, bring out your queen knight and push your d pawn two. Now take with your c pawn. [%cal Yc5d4] } 3... cxd4 { You trade a wing pawn for his middle pawn, and your c column opens. [%csl Gd4] } (3... d5 { Pushing past lets him open the middle for his pieces. } 4. exd5 exd5 5. Bb5+ Nc6 6. O-O { Slightly better for him: your king is still in the middle, and his bishop pins your knight. [%cal Rb5e8] }) 4. Nxd4 { His knight takes back and stands in the middle. Now the quiet move that gives this line its name: pawn to a6. [%cal Ya7a6] } 4... a6 { That is the Kan: after the pawns trade in the middle, your a pawn steps to a6, so his knight and bishop can never use b5. [%csl Gb5] } (4... Nc6 { Just as good: the knight comes out first, a different Sicilian. } 5. Nc3 Qc7 { About level: another sound set-up, where his knight may still use b5. [%csl Bb5] }) (4... Nf6 { Just as good: the knight hits e4 at once. } 5. Nc3 Nc6 { About level: your knight hits e4, and his knight came to c3 to guard it. [%cal Gf6e4] }) 5. Bd3 { His bishop comes out and guards e4. Bring your dark bishop out to c5, where it hits his knight. [%cal Rd3e4,Yf8c5] } 5... Bc5 { The bishop hits his knight on d4, and behind the knight the same line runs on to f2. [%cal Gc5f2] } (5... Nf6 { Just as good: the knight comes out first. } 6. O-O Qc7 { About level: a slower set-up, with your queen on c7 and your knight on f6. [%csl Bf6] }) (5... Qc7 { Just as good: the queen comes out first. } 6. O-O Nc6 { About level: your queen and knight both watch e5. [%cal Gc7e5,Gc6e5] }) (5... Nc6 { Just as good: the knight first, offering a trade. } 6. Nxc6 dxc6 { About level: the knights came off, and your queen can use the open d column. [%cal Gd8d3] }) 6. Nb3 { His knight steps back to b3 and hits your bishop. Tuck it into the corner: bishop to a7. [%cal Yc5a7] } 6... Ba7 { From a7 the bishop still aims at f2, and no pawn of his can chase it there. [%cal Ga7f2] } (6... Be7 { Just as good: the bishop steps back to a quieter square. } 7. O-O Nc6 { About level: the bishop is safe on e7, but it no longer aims at f2. [%csl Be7] }) 7. Qe2 { His queen steps up to guard e4. Against castling or his knight to c3, the same knight move follows. Now bring out your queen knight. [%cal Re2e4,Yb8c6] } 7... Nc6 { That is the Kan set-up: your bishop and knight both watch d4, and his knight needed three moves to land on b3, far from the middle. [%cal Ga7d4,Gc6d4] } (7... Ne7 { Just as good: from there the knight can go to c6 or g6. [%cal Ge7c6,Ge7g6] } 8. Be3 O-O 9. Bxa7 Rxa7 { About level: the dark bishops are gone, and your king is castled. [%csl Gg8] }) (7... d6 { Just as good: the pawn frees your light bishop. } 8. Be3 Nf6 { About level: your knight hits e4, and your d pawn guards e5. [%cal Gf6e4,Gd6e5] }) 0-1


[Event "Sicilian Defence: his queen hits g7"]
[Result "0-1"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/0Jbuvv6d/vd5w08of"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[FEN "rnbqk1nr/bp1p1ppp/p3p3/8/4P3/1N1B4/PPP2PPP/RNBQK2R w KQkq - 4 7"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/rnbqk1nr/bp1p1ppp/p3p3/8/4P3/1N1B4/PPP2PPP/RNBQK2R_w_KQkq_-_4_7"]

{ Your bishop sits on a7, aiming at f2, and his knight has been pushed far from the middle. [%cal Ga7f2] }
7. Qg4 { His queen jumps out and hits your pawn on g7. It looks as if that pawn needs a guard. Does it? [%csl Rg7] } 7... Nf6 { Right: g7 is bait. If his queen takes it, your rook chases her, and your bishop takes f2 with check. [%cal Ga7f2] } (7... Nc6 { Your usual knight move, but now g7 simply falls: the knight on g8 still blocks your rook. } 8. Qxg7 { Better for him: his queen took g7 and hits your rook on h8, still shut in by your knight. [%cal Rg7h8] }) (7... Ne7 { The knight clears g8, but from e7 it helps nothing against his queen. } 8. Qxg7 Rg8 9. Qxh7 { Better for him: his queen took two pawns, and your knight on e7 does nothing against her. [%csl Rg7,Rh7] }) (7... d6 { The pawn move ignores g7. } 8. Qxg7 Qf6 9. Qxf6 Nxf6 { Better for him: the queens came off, but he took g7 on the way, a clean pawn up. [%csl Rg7] }) (7... Qf6 { The queen guards g7, but she takes the square your knight wants. [%cal Bf6g7] } 8. f4 { Slightly better for him: your queen guards g7 but blocks your knight, and his pawns line up against her. [%cal Rf6g7][%csl Rf6] }) (7... Kf8 { The king guards g7, but it gives up castling. [%cal Bf8g7] } 8. Qe2 Nc6 9. Nc3 { Slightly better for him: g7 is safe, but your king has lost castling and your rook on h8 is shut in. [%csl Rf8] }) (7... g6 { The g pawn steps up, but it leaves holes on f6 and h6. } 8. Qg3 Nf6 9. Bg5 { Better for him: his bishop lands next to your knight, and the dark squares around your king are weak. [%csl Rf6,Rh6] }) 8. Qg3 { His queen steps back to g3, still eyeing g7. Look at his e pawn before you spend the move. [%csl Re4] } 8... d6 { Right: the pawn guards e5, so his e pawn can never kick your knight, and g7 is still bait. The Kan holds firm. [%cal Gd6e5] } (8... e5 { Your pawn takes e5 first, but nothing guards it there. } 9. Qxe5+ Qe7 10. Qxe7+ Kxe7 { Better for him: his queen took e5 with check, and after the queen trade you are a pawn down. [%csl Re5] }) (8... Nc6 { Natural, but it lets his e pawn kick your knight. } 9. e5 Nh5 10. Qh3 g6 11. g4 Ng7 { Slightly better for him: your knight was chased all the way to g7, and it has no good square left. [%csl Rg7] }) (8... O-O { Castling puts your king where his queen and bishop aim. } 9. Bg5 Nh5 10. Qh4 { Slightly better for him: his queen and bishop press on your king's side, and your knight had to go to the edge. [%csl Rh5] }) 0-1


[Event "Sicilian Defence: he clamps with c4"]
[Result "0-1"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/0Jbuvv6d/pX3pKykM"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/1p1p1ppp/p3p3/8/3NP3/8/PPP2PPP/RNBQKB1R w KQkq - 0 5"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/rnbqkbnr/1p1p1ppp/p3p3/8/3NP3/8/PPP2PPP/RNBQKB1R_w_KQkq_-_0_5"]

{ The Kan: your pawn on a6, his knight in the middle on d4. }
5. c4 { He pushes his c pawn too: his two pawns squeeze the d5 square between them. Start the fight against his clamp. [%csl Rd5] } 5... Nf6 { Right: the knight hits e4, which has no guard yet, so he must spend a move on it. [%cal Gf6e4] } (5... Bb4+ { Just as good: the check comes first. } 6. Bd2 Qb6 { About level: your queen hits his knight on d4, which lost its queen's guard when his bishop came to d2. [%cal Gb6d4] }) (5... Qc7 { Just as good: the queen watches c4 and e5 before your knight comes out. [%cal Gc7c4,Gc7e5] } 6. Nc3 Nf6 { About level: your knight hits e4 a move later, and your queen already watches c4. [%cal Gc7c4] }) (5... Nc6 { Natural, but it lets him trade knights and split your pawns. } 6. Nc3 Bc5 7. Nxc6 bxc6 { About level, but your queen side pawns are split, and his clamp still holds d5. [%csl Ra6,Rc6] }) 6. Nc3 { His knight comes out to guard e4. Had his bishop come to d3 instead, your queen knight would hit his knight in the middle. Keep the pressure on. [%cal Rc3e4] } 6... Bb4 { Right: the bishop pins his knight to his king, so e4 is guarded only on paper. [%cal Gb4e1] } (6... Bc5 { Your usual bishop move, but now nothing stops his e pawn. } 7. e5 Qb6 8. Be3 { Better for him: his pawn hits your knight, and his bishop on e3 shuts your queen and bishop off from d4. [%csl Rf6] }) (6... e5 { The pawn kicks his knight, but it leaves a hole on d5. } 7. Nf5 d6 { Slightly better for him: his knight sits on f5, and d5 is a hole in front of your pawn. [%csl Rf5,Rd5] }) (6... Bd6 { Natural, but his knight jumps at your bishop. } 7. Nf5 { Better for him: his knight hits your bishop and your pawn on g7 at once. [%cal Rf5d6,Rf5g7] }) (6... Nc6 { Natural, but he trades knights and your queen comes off the board. } 7. Nxc6 dxc6 8. Qxd8+ Kxd8 { Slightly better for him: the queens came off, and your king had to take back on d8, stuck in the middle. [%csl Rd8] }) (6... Be7 { Natural, but it leaves your knight open to his e pawn. } 7. e5 Ng8 { Better for him: his pawn drove your knight back to its starting square. [%csl Rg8] }) (6... Qc7 { Just as good, and the choice of most players: your queen watches c4 and e5. [%cal Gc7c4,Gc7e5] } 7. a3 b6 { About level: a slower game, and his pawn on a3 keeps your bishop off b4. [%csl Ba3] }) (6... d6 { Solid, but it leaves his clamp alone. } 7. Be3 Nc6 8. Be2 { Slightly better for him: your pawn on d6 is solid, but his clamp stands and his pieces come out freely. [%csl Rd5] }) (6... d5 { Just as good: the pawn strikes at his clamp at once. } 7. cxd5 exd5 8. exd5 Nxd5 { About level: his clamp is gone, and the pawns in the middle came off. [%csl Gd5] }) 7. Bd3 { His bishop guards e4 a second time. Had his queen come to d3 instead, your d pawn would crack the clamp at once. Careful: your dark bishop has no guard. [%cal Rd3e4][%csl Rb4] } 7... Nc6 { Right: the knight hits his knight on d4 and blocks the road from a4 to your king, so no queen check there can win your bishop. [%cal Gc6d4] } (7... Qb6 { The queen guards your bishop, but his bishop comes to e3 with a hit on her. } 8. Be3 Bxc3+ 9. bxc3 { Slightly better for him: his bishop drove your queen, and your dark bishop had to go for his knight. [%csl Re3] }) (7... Qa5 { The queen guards your bishop, but his bishop steps to d2 and breaks the pin. } 8. Bd2 Qc7 9. O-O { Slightly better for him: the pin is gone, and your queen had to move again. [%csl Rd2] }) (7... a5 { The pawn guards your bishop, but his e pawn kicks your knight. } 8. e5 Ng8 { Much better for him: his e pawn chased your knight home to g8, and your pieces are stuck. [%csl Rg8] }) (7... d6 { Solid, but it leaves your bishop on b4 loose. } 8. Qa4+ Nc6 9. Nxc6 Bxc3+ 10. bxc3 Qd7 11. Ba3 bxc6 { Better for him: the queen check from a4 cost you your dark bishop, and your pawns are torn apart. [%cal Ra4e8] }) (7... O-O { Castling lets his e pawn kick your knight. } 8. e5 Ne8 9. O-O { Slightly better for him: his pawn drove your knight back to e8, and your pieces are tangled. [%csl Re8] }) (7... d5 { Striking at once, but your bishop on b4 is loose. } 8. Qa4+ Nc6 9. exd5 { Better for him: a pawn down, and your pinned knight on c6 is attacked twice. [%cal Ra4e8][%csl Rc6] }) (7... Bxc3+ { Just as good: your bishop breaks up his pawns. } 8. bxc3 d6 { About level: his c pawns are doubled, and you gave up your bishop for it. [%csl Rc3,Rc4] }) (7... Qc7 { Playable: from c7 the queen eyes his pawn on c4. [%cal Gc7c4] } 8. O-O Bxc3 9. bxc3 d6 { About level: you traded your bishop for his knight, and your queen on c7 watches his pawn on c4. [%cal Gc7c4] }) 8. Nxc6 { He trades knights. Against a retreat to c2 you would take his other knight and push your d pawn; against e2, your bishop drops back. Two pawns can take back. Which one? } 8... dxc6 { Right: the d pawn takes, and the d column opens for your queen, straight at his bishop on d3. [%cal Gd8d3] } (8... bxc6 { The b pawn takes, but it leaves holes on c5 and b6 for his pieces. } 9. O-O e5 10. Na4 { Slightly better for him: his knight heads for the holes on c5 and b6, and no pawn of yours can chase it. [%csl Rc5,Rb6] }) (8... Bxc3+ { The check first, but now the recapture comes too late. } 9. bxc3 dxc6 10. e5 Nd7 11. Bf4 { Better for him: his pawn kicked your knight back, and your dark bishop is gone. [%csl Rd7] }) 9. O-O { He castles, and his e pawn is one step from your knight. How do you finish the job against his clamp? } 9... e5 { Right: the pawn takes d4 and f4 away from his pieces, and your queen owns the open d column. Had his queen come out instead, this push fits just as well. [%cal Ge5d4,Ge5f4] } (9... Qxd3 { Taking looks free, but look at his queen on d1. } 10. Qxd3 { Your queen is gone for his bishop: his queen was guarding d3 all along. [%cal Rd1d3] }) (9... O-O { Castling first lets his e pawn jump at your knight. } 10. e5 Nd7 11. Bf4 { Slightly better for him: his pawn chased your knight to d7, and his bishop holds the pawn on e5. [%cal Rf4e5] }) (9... Bd7 { The bishop comes out, but his e pawn jumps at your knight. } 10. e5 Ng8 11. Qg4 { Better for him: your knight had to go home to g8, and his queen already eyes g7. [%cal Rg4g7] }) (9... Bxc3 { Just as good: you break up his pawns first. } 10. bxc3 e5 { Level: his c pawns are doubled, and your pawn on e5 holds d4. [%csl Rc3] }) (9... Qc7 { Just as good: the queen comes to c7 first. } 10. Be3 Bxc3 11. bxc3 e5 { About level: your queen and pawn both hold e5, and his c pawns are doubled. [%csl Rc3] }) 0-1


[Event "Sicilian Defence: his pawn hits your knight"]
