[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bqk2r/1p3ppp/p1p1pn2/8/1bP1P3/2NB4/PP3PPP/R1BQK2R_w_KQkq_-_0_9"]

{ His knight has traded itself off on c6, and your bishop pins his knight on c3. }
9. e5 { His pawn hits your knight. Moving it away is the obvious answer. Is there a stronger one? [%csl Rf6] } 9... Qa5 { Right: a counterattack. Your queen hits e5, and if he takes your knight, your bishop takes c3 with check. [%cal Ga5e5] } (9... O-O { Castling leaves your knight to his pawn. } 10. exf6 Qxf6 { A knight for a pawn: his pawn took your knight while you castled. [%csl Rf6] }) (9... Qxd3 { Look again: his queen guards d3. [%cal Rd1d3] } 10. Qxd3 { A queen for a bishop: his queen took yours back, and your knight on f6 is still attacked. [%csl Rf6] }) (9... Qd4 { Just as good: from d4 your queen hits e5 and his pinned knight. [%cal Gd4e5,Gd4c3] } 10. exf6 Bxc3+ 11. bxc3 Qxc3+ 12. Bd2 Qxd3 { Level: the check and the fork won the pieces back, though his pawn on f6 still eyes g7. [%csl Rf6] }) (9... Nd7 { The natural retreat, but his pawns gain room. } 10. f4 Nc5 11. Be2 { About level, but his pawns on e5 and f4 cramp your pieces, and the counterattack is gone. [%csl Re5,Rf4] }) (9... Bxc3+ { The bishop takes first, so there is no check left to win anything back. } 10. bxc3 Nd7 11. Bf4 { Better for him: your knight had to retreat, and his bishops have open roads. [%csl Rd7] }) (9... Nd5 { Look again: his c pawn takes a knight on d5. } 10. cxd5 exd5 { A knight for a pawn: his c pawn took it, and your pawn only took a pawn back. [%csl Rd5] }) (9... Ng4 { The knight jumps to g4, where his queen takes it. } 10. Qxg4 Qxd3 11. Qxg7 { Better for him: you are a pawn down, and his queen is loose among your pieces. [%csl Rg7] }) 10. exf6 { He takes your knight. For the moment you are a piece down. [%csl Rf6] } 10... Bxc3+ { Right: the check comes first, so he has no time to save anything. [%cal Gb4c3] } (10... gxf6 { Taking back on f6 lets him save his pieces. } 11. Bd2 { A knight for a pawn: his bishop covered his knight, and nothing of his hangs now. [%csl Rf6] }) (10... Qe5+ { A check, but the wrong one: he blocks it, and your knight is gone for good. } 11. Be2 Qxf6 { A knight for a pawn: his bishop blocked the check, and your queen only won back the pawn. [%csl Rf6] }) 11. bxc3 { He takes back on c3 with his b pawn. } 11... Qxc3+ { Right: the queen takes with check and hits his rook on a1 and his bishop on d3 at the same time. [%cal Gc3e1,Gc3a1,Gc3d3] } 12. Bd2 { He blocks the check with his bishop, not his queen. Had his queen blocked on d2, yours would simply take the rook on a1. What is different now? } 12... Qxd3 { Right: his bishop on d2 blocks his own queen's guard of d3, so your queen takes the bishop for free. [%cal Rd1d3] } (12... Qxa1 { Look again: his queen guards a1 along the back row. [%cal Rd1a1] } 13. Qxa1 { A queen for a rook: his queen simply took yours back. Better for him. [%cal Rd1a1] }) (12... Qxf6 { Taking the pawn lets his bishop on d3 escape. } 13. O-O { Better for him: his bishop got away, and he keeps a bishop against your two pawns. [%csl Rd3] }) 13. fxg7 { His pawn takes on g7 and hits your rook on h8. [%cal Rg7h8] } 13... Rg8 { Right: the rook steps to g8 and stops his pawn, and the material is level again, piece for piece and pawn for pawn. If his bishop leaves that pawn unguarded, check with your queen first. [%csl Gg8] } (13... Qxd2+ { Look again: his queen takes back on d2. } 14. Qxd2 { A queen for a bishop: his queen took yours back. Better for him. [%csl Rd2] }) (13... Qe4+ { A check first, but his bishop blocks it with a gain of time. } 14. Be3 Rg8 15. Qd4 Qxd4 16. Bxd4 { Better for him: the queens came off, and his bishop now guards the pawn on g7, which ties your rook down. [%cal Rd4g7] }) 0-1


