{ His knight is on f3 and your pawn on e6. He has not pushed his d pawn yet. }
3. c3 { He plays c3, ready to follow with d4 and a full pawn centre. What do you do about it? [%csl Rd4] } 3... d5 { Right: your pawn hits e4 before d4 arrives. [%cal Gd5e4] } (3... d6 { Solid, but it lets him build his centre. } 4. d4 { Slightly better for him: he got his full pawn centre, d4 next to e4. [%csl Rd4,Re4] }) (3... Be7 { Developing, but it lets him build his centre. } 4. d4 { About level, but he got his full centre with d4 at no cost. [%csl Rd4] }) (3... Nf6 { Just as good: the knight hits e4 first. } 4. e5 Nd5 5. d4 { Level: his pawn chased your knight to d5, a fine square, and a different kind of game. [%csl Gd5] }) (3... Nc6 { Just as good: the knight first, and d5 can follow. } 4. d4 d5 { About level: your pawn reached d5 a move later, with the knight already out. [%csl Gd5] }) 4. exd5 { He takes on d5. Had he pushed e5 instead, your pawn would push past him. Which piece takes back? } 4... Qxd5 { Right: the queen stands in the middle, and his c3 pawn has taken the square his knight would use to chase her. [%csl Gc3] } (4... exd5 { Just as good: the e pawn takes, and your pieces get open lines. } 5. d4 Nc6 { Level: an open game, with your pawn on d5 and the e column open. [%csl Gd5] }) (4... Nf6 { Natural, but it leaves his pawn on d5 alive. } 5. Bb5+ Bd7 6. Bxd7+ Qxd7 7. dxe6 Qxe6+ 8. Qe2 { A pawn down: he took two pawns, and you won back only one. [%csl Re6] }) 5. d4 { He pushes d4 after all, backed by his pawn on c3. } 5... Nf6 { Right: the knight comes out and guards your queen on d5. [%cal Gf6d5] } (5... Bd6 { Natural, but it lets his c pawn chase your queen. } 6. c4 Qc6 7. Nc3 { Slightly better for him: his c pawn chased your queen and gave his knight the c3 square back. [%csl Rc3] }) (5... Nc6 { Just as good: the other knight first. } 6. Bd3 Nf6 { About level: both knights are out, and your queen stands safe in the middle. [%csl Gd5] }) (5... cxd4 { Trading first gives his knight the c3 square back. } 6. cxd4 Nf6 7. Nc3 Qd8 { About level: his knight came to c3 with a hit on your queen, and she went home. [%csl Rc3] }) 6. Na3 { His knight comes to a3. Against a bishop move instead, bring your bishop to e7 and castle. Where does his knight want to go? [%cal Ra3b5] } 6... a6 { Right: the Kan pawn shuts his knight out of b5, and your queen stands safe in the middle, since no knight of his can reach c3. [%csl Gb5] } (6... Bd6 { Natural, but his pieces start chasing your queen and bishop. } 7. Bc4 Qe4+ 8. Be2 O-O 9. Nb5 { Slightly better for him: his knight jumped to b5 and hits your bishop on d6. [%cal Rb5d6] }) (6... Nc6 { Just as good, but b5 stays open for his knight. } 7. Be3 { About level: your knight is out, and his bishop guards d4, with b5 still open to his knight. [%cal Re3d4,Ra3b5] }) (6... Qd8 { Just as good: the queen steps back before his knight can hit her. } 7. Nc4 Nc6 { Level: your queen is safe at home, and your pieces come out. [%csl Gd8] }) (6... cxd4 { Just as good: the trade comes first. } 7. Nb5 Na6 8. Nbxd4 { About level: the pawns came off, and your knight stands on the edge at a6. [%csl Ra6] }) (6... Be7 { Natural, but his knight jumps to b5 with a threat on c7. } 7. Nb5 Na6 { About level: his knight hit c7, and your knight went to the edge at a6 to cover it. [%cal Rb5c7] }) 0-1


[Event "Sicilian Defence: the queen side march"]
[Result "0-1"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/0Jbuvv6d/LIvRIBET"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/1p1p1ppp/p3p3/8/3NP3/8/PPP2PPP/RNBQKB1R w KQkq - 0 5"]
[SetUp "1"]
