{ The Kan: your pawn on a6 keeps his pieces off b5, and his knight stands in the middle. }
5. Nc3 { His knight comes to c3. Now use the room your a pawn made: push your b pawn to b5. [%cal Yb7b5] } 5... b5 { Your b pawn grabs space on the queen side, guarded by the pawn on a6, and your light bishop gets a new road through b7. [%cal Gc8b7] } (5... Qc7 { Just as good, and the most played: the queen comes to c7. } 6. Bd3 Nc6 { About level: a flexible set-up, with your queen on c7 watching e5. [%cal Gc7e5] }) (5... Nc6 { Just as good: the knight first. } 6. Nxc6 bxc6 { About level: the knights came off, and two of your pawns guard d5. [%cal Gc6d5,Ge6d5] }) 6. Bd3 { His bishop comes out. Had he pushed a3 to stop your b pawn, your bishop would come out the same way. Now bring your light bishop to b7. [%cal Yc8b7] } 6... Bb7 { That is the long diagonal: the corner-to-corner road from a8 to h1. Your bishop looks down it at his king pawn. [%cal Gb7e4] } (6... Qb6 { Just as good, and the most played: the queen hits his knight. } 7. Nb3 Qc7 { About level: his knight stepped back, and your queen settles on c7. [%csl Gc7] }) 7. O-O { He castles. Now your queen knight comes to c6 and offers a trade. [%cal Yb8c6] } 7... Nc6 { The knight hits his knight on d4. If he trades, your bishop takes back and stays on the long diagonal. [%cal Gc6d4] } (7... b4 { Just as good: your b pawn kicks his knight to the edge. } 8. Na4 Nc6 { About level: his knight sits on the edge at a4, and yours comes to c6. [%csl Ra4] }) (7... Qc7 { Natural, but his rook comes to e1 while your king waits in the middle. } 8. Re1 Be7 9. e5 { Slightly better for him: his rook and pawn press down the e column while your king waits on e8. [%csl Re8] }) (7... d6 { Solid, but it gives him time for a4. } 8. a4 b4 9. Na2 { Slightly better for him: his a pawn pressed your b pawn on to b4, where his knight attacks it. [%cal Ra2b4][%csl Rb4] }) (7... Nf6 { The knight comes out, but his e pawn kicks it at once. } 8. e5 Nd5 9. Nxd5 Bxd5 { About level: the knights came off, and your bishop landed on d5. [%csl Gd5] }) 8. Nxc6 { He trades knights. Against bishop e3, your other knight comes to f6. Take back with the bishop, so it stays on the long diagonal. [%cal Yb7c6] } 8... Bxc6 { That is the queen side march: your pawns hold the queen side, and your bishop on the long diagonal hits e4. [%cal Gc6e4] } (8... dxc6 { The pawn takes, but it shuts your bishop in behind it. } 9. e5 { Slightly better for him: your bishop is shut in behind the pawn on c6, and his pawn on e5 cramps you. [%csl Rb7] }) 0-1


