{ Your c pawn stands on c5, facing his king pawn. }
2. d4 { He pushes d4 at once, right next to your c pawn. } 2... cxd4 { Right: you take his middle pawn with your wing pawn. [%cal Gc5d4] } (2... e6 { The Kan pawn move, but it lets his pawn push past. } 3. d5 Nf6 4. Nc3 { About level, but his pawn on d5 cramps your pieces, and he has more room in the middle. [%csl Rd5] }) (2... Nf6 { The knight comes out, but his e pawn chases it. } 3. e5 Nd5 4. c4 Nc7 5. dxc5 { Slightly better for him: his pawns chased your knight back to c7, and he took your c pawn on the way. [%csl Rc5] }) (2... Nc6 { The knight comes out, but his pawn pushes past and chases it. } 3. d5 Nb8 { Better for him: his pawn drove your knight straight back, and he owns the middle. [%csl Rb8] }) 3. c3 { He offers you a pawn. Take it, and he gets time to bring every piece out quickly. Is the pawn worth it? } 3... dxc3 { Right: you accept, and his knight must spend a move taking back. [%csl Gc3] } (3... d5 { Declining with a strike in the middle. } 4. exd5 Qxd5 5. cxd4 { Level: you gave the pawn back, and his d pawn now stands alone in the middle, with no pawn beside it. [%csl Bd4] }) (3... e6 { Declining, but his centre comes back. } 4. cxd4 d5 5. e5 { About level: you declined, and he rebuilt his centre with pawns on d4 and e5. [%csl Rd4,Re5] }) (3... Nf6 { Declining is playable: the knight hits e4. } 4. e5 Nd5 { Level, a little less than taking: his pawn chased your knight to d5, and your extra pawn on d4 is under fire. [%csl Rd4] }) (3... d3 { Pushing past gives the pawn back without a fight. } 4. Bxd3 Nc6 { About level, but you gave the pawn back, and his bishop came out to d3 for free. [%csl Rd3] }) 4. Nxc3 { His knight takes back on c3. You are a pawn up. Start your development. } 4... Nc6 { Right: the knight comes out and watches d4 and e5. [%cal Gc6d4,Gc6e5] } (4... Nf6 { Developing the knight, but his e pawn is ready to kick it. } 5. e5 Ng8 { Slightly better for him: you are a pawn up, but his pawn drove your knight back to g8 and his pieces are out first. [%csl Rg8] }) (4... e6 { Just as good: the pawn first, opening your bishop's road. } 5. Nf3 d6 { Level: you are a pawn up, and his quicker development makes up for it. [%csl Gd6] }) (4... d6 { Just as good: the pawn guards e5 first. [%cal Gd6e5] } 5. Nf3 e6 { Level: a pawn up, with his pieces a step ahead of yours. [%csl Ge6] }) 5. Nf3 { His king knight comes out. Had his bishop come to c4 first, the game goes the same way once his knight reaches f3. } 5... e6 { Right: the pawn on e6 guards d5 and opens your dark bishop's road. [%cal Ge6d5] } (5... d5 { Opening the middle helps his quicker pieces. } 6. exd5 Nb8 { Much better for him: his pawn took on d5 and chased your knight home, and his pieces are far ahead. [%csl Rd5] }) (5... d6 { Just as good: the d pawn moves before the e pawn. } 6. Bc4 e6 { Level: your pawns on d6 and e6 blunt his bishop, and you stay a pawn up. [%cal Rc4f7] }) 6. Bc4 { His bishop comes out and aims at e6 and the f7 square behind it. What now? [%cal Rc4f7] } 6... a6 { Right: the Kan pawn keeps his knight off b5 and prepares b5 against his bishop. [%csl Gb5] } (6... Nf6 { Just as good: the knight comes out. } 7. O-O { Level: a pawn up, with your knight out on f6. [%csl Gf6] }) (6... d5 { Opening the middle gives your extra pawn back. } 7. exd5 exd5 8. Nxd5 { Slightly better for him: the pawns came off, and his knight on d5 took back your extra pawn. [%csl Rd5] }) (6... Bb4 { Just as good: the bishop pins his knight on c3. } 7. O-O Nge7 { Level: your bishop hits his knight, and you are still a pawn up. [%cal Gb4c3] }) (6... d6 { Just as good: your d pawn steps up to guard e5. [%cal Gd6e5] } 7. Qe2 Be7 { Level: a pawn up, with pawns on d6 and e6 in front of his bishop. [%csl Gd6] }) (6... Qc7 { Natural, but his knight can jump to b5 and hit your queen. } 7. O-O Nf6 8. Nb5 Qb8 { About level: his knight on b5 chased your queen to b8, so your extra pawn buys little. [%csl Rb5] }) 7. O-O { He castles, and his bishop still sits on c4. } 7... b5 { Right: the pawn hits his bishop and gains room on the queen side. [%cal Gb5c4] } (7... Nf6 { The knight comes out, but his e pawn is ready for it. } 8. e5 d5 9. exf6 dxc4 10. Qxd8+ Kxd8 { About level: you are a pawn up, but the queens came off and your king stands in the middle. [%csl Rd8] }) (7... d5 { Opening the middle while your king is on e8. } 8. exd5 Na5 9. dxe6 fxe6 { Much better for him: the pawns came off in the middle, and your king is stuck on e8 in the open. [%csl Re8] }) (7... Nge7 { Just as good, and the most played: the knight heads for g6. } 8. Bg5 h6 9. Be3 { Level: your knight is out, and his bishop was pushed back. [%csl Gh6] }) (7... d6 { Just as good: the pawn to d6 first. } 8. Qe2 Be7 { Level: a solid set-up with the extra pawn. [%csl Ge7] }) (7... Qc7 { Natural, but it walks into a knight jump on d5. } 8. Nd5 exd5 9. exd5 Na5 10. Re1+ Be7 11. Bf1 { About level: you are a knight up, but your king is stuck in the middle and his rook owns the open e column. [%csl Re8] }) 8. Bb3 { His bishop steps back, still aiming at e6 and f7. [%cal Rb3f7] } 8... Bb7 { Right: your bishop comes to the long diagonal and hits e4. [%cal Gb7e4] } (8... Be7 { Natural, but his knight jumps into d5. } 9. Nd5 Nf6 10. Bf4 d6 11. Nxf6+ Bxf6 12. Bxd6 { Level: his knight jump won your extra pawn back on d6. [%csl Rd6] }) (8... Nf6 { The knight comes out, but his e pawn kicks it. } 9. e5 Ng8 { Slightly better for him: you are a pawn up, but your knight is back home and you are far behind in development. [%csl Rg8] }) (8... Na5 { Just as good: the knight hits his bishop. } 9. Bc2 Bb7 { Slightly better for you: a pawn up, with your bishop on the long diagonal. [%csl Gb7] }) (8... Nge7 { A little worse: his bishop comes to e3 with gain of time. } 9. Be3 Na5 10. Rc1 { Level: your knight is out, but his pieces are all in play for the pawn. [%csl Re3] }) (8... d6 { Solid, but it gives his pieces time and targets. } 9. Qe2 Nge7 10. Rd1 Ng6 11. e5 Ncxe5 12. Nxe5 Nxe5 { Level: you are two pawns up, but his pieces are all active and your king is still in the middle. [%csl Re8] }) 9. Qe2 { His queen comes to e2, backing up his e pawn. He could also have tried a bishop move or a rook move. } 9... Nge7 { Right: your knight comes out on its way to g6, and you stand a pawn up. Against the bishop move this is right too; against a rook move, your bishop comes to c5 first. [%csl Ge7] } (9... Nf6 { The knight comes out, but his e pawn kicks it back. } 10. e5 Ng8 { About level: you are a pawn up, but his pawn drove your knight back to g8. [%csl Rg8] }) (9... Bd6 { Natural, but his rook hits it at once. } 10. Rd1 Bb4 { About level: you are a pawn up, but his rook on d1 made your bishop move twice. [%csl Rd1] }) (9... Bc5 { Just as good: the bishop comes out to c5. } 10. e5 Na5 11. Bc2 { Level: a pawn up, and your knight hits his bishop. [%csl Ga5] }) (9... Na5 { Playable: the knight goes after his bishop on b3, though the bishops come off. } 10. Bd5 Qb8 11. Bxb7 Qxb7 { Level: the light bishops came off, and you keep the extra pawn. [%csl Gb7] }) (9... Qc7 { Natural, but his knight can still jump into d5. } 10. Nd5 exd5 11. exd5+ Nce7 { Slightly better for him: you are a knight up, but the e column is open and your king is still on e8, facing his queen. [%csl Re8] }) (9... d6 { Solid, but it gives him time for rook d1. } 10. Rd1 Na5 11. Bc2 { About level: your extra pawn on d6 is under pressure from his rook. [%csl Rd6] }) 10. Bg5 { His bishop comes out and eyes your knight on e7. Against a rook move instead, your knight would go on to g6. } 10... Na5 { Right: the knight hits his bishop on b3, the piece aiming at your king's side, and you stay a pawn up with your bishop on the long diagonal. [%cal Ga5b3] } (10... h6 { Just as good, and the most played: the pawn chases his bishop. } 11. Be3 Na5 { Slightly better for you: a pawn up, and your knight now hits his bishop on b3. [%cal Ga5b3] }) (10... f6 { Just as good, but it invites a wild fight. } 11. Rad1 fxg5 12. Bxe6 dxe6 13. Rxd8+ Rxd8 { Slightly better for you: after the trades, your rook and two bishops outweigh his queen. [%csl Gd8] }) 0-1


