{ Your pawns stand on c5 and e6, and his king knight is out. }
3. Nc3 { His queen knight comes out before his d pawn moves. } 3... a6 { The Kan pawn takes b5 from his pieces before anything else. [%csl Gb5] } (3... Nc6 { Just as good: your queen knight comes out before the a pawn moves. } 4. d4 cxd4 5. Nxd4 { About level: another Sicilian, with your knight out on c6 and your a pawn still at home. [%csl Bc6] }) 4. d4 { He opens the middle with d4. He could also have played g3, a slower plan. } 4... cxd4 { You trade your wing pawn for his middle pawn. } (4... b5 { Too early: his pawn pushes past to d5. } 5. d5 d6 6. dxe6 fxe6 { Slightly better for him: your pawn on e6 is weak, and your king side is loose. [%csl Re6] }) 5. Nxd4 { His knight takes back. } 5... b5 { That is the queen side march, reached by another road: your pawns hold the queen side and open b7 for your bishop. Against the slower g3, your b pawn and bishop come out the same way. [%csl Gb5] } (5... Qc7 { Just as good, and what most players choose: queen to c7. } 6. Bd3 Nf6 { About level: a flexible set-up, with your queen watching e5 and your knight out. [%cal Gc7e5] }) 0-1


