{ Your knight is out on f6, and his queen still eyes your pawn on g7. }
8. Qxg7 { He grabs the pawn on g7, and his queen now hits your rook in the corner. The grab is a mistake: queen back to g3 was his move. Show him why. [%cal Rg7h8] } 8... Rg8 { Right: your rook leaves the corner with a hit on his queen, so she has to move again. [%cal Gg8g7] } (8... Bxf2+ { Just as good: the check first, and your rook chases her next. [%cal Ga7f2] } 9. Kd1 Rg8 10. Qh6 { Level: the same two moves in the other order, and his king on d1 has lost castling. [%csl Bd1] }) (8... Rf8 { The rook hides on f8, but his e pawn is ready to kick your knight. } 9. e5 Ng8 10. Bxh7 Nc6 11. Bxg8 { Much better for him: his e pawn drove your knight back to g8, and his bishop took h7 and then the knight. [%csl Rg8] }) (8... Nc6 { Your usual knight move, but the rook in the corner is still hanging. [%csl Rh8] } 9. Qxh8+ Ke7 10. Qxd8+ Nxd8 { A rook and a pawn down: his queen took the rook with check, then traded herself for yours. [%cal Rg7h8] }) (8... d6 { The pawn guards e5, but your rook in the corner still hangs. [%cal Gd6e5] } 9. Qxh8+ Ke7 10. Qg7 { You have lost the rook: his queen took it with check and still sits among your pieces. [%csl Rg7] }) (8... Qa5+ { A check first? His knight on b3 guards a5. [%cal Rb3a5] } 9. Nxa5 { Your queen is lost to his knight, and your rook in the corner still hangs. [%csl Ra5] }) 9. Qh6 { His queen runs to h6, the one square where nothing takes her. You are a pawn down, for now. } 9... Bxf2+ { Right: your bishop takes f2 with check, and the pawns are level again. [%cal Ga7f2] } (9... Rxg2 { Taking a pawn back looks natural, but his e pawn is ready to jump. [%cal Re4e5] } 10. e5 Ng8 11. Qxh7 { Better for him: his pawn chased your knight home, and his queen took h7 on the way. [%csl Rh7] }) (9... Rg6 { The rook chases her a second time, but she still has room. } 10. Qh4 { Slightly better for him: his queen stepped aside to h4, and you are still a pawn down. [%csl Rh4] }) (9... Ng4 { The knight hits his queen, but she eats a pawn on the way out. } 10. Qxh7 Rf8 { Better for him: his queen took h7 too, and your rook had to run from her. [%csl Rh7] }) (9... Nc6 { Bringing the knight out is natural, but it gives him a free move. } 10. Be3 { Slightly better for him: his bishop now blocks the road to f2 and offers a trade of bishops. [%cal Re3a7] }) (9... d6 { Solid, but it gives his king time to hide. } 10. O-O { About level, but you are a pawn down, and his king has castled out of reach. [%csl Rg1] }) (9... e5 { The pawn grabs space, but it is slow here. } 10. h3 { Slightly better for him: his pawn took g4 away from your knight, and his extra pawn stays. [%csl Rg4] }) (9... d5 { Striking in the middle, but his e pawn jumps first. } 10. e5 Ng4 11. Qxh7 Kf8 { Better for him: your knight was kicked, his queen collected h7, and your king had to guard the rook. [%csl Rf8] }) (9... Qb6 { The queen aims at f2, but she was the only guard of your knight. } 10. Qxf6 { A knight and a pawn down: his queen took the knight for free. [%csl Rf6] }) (9... Qa5+ { Look again: his knight on b3 guards a5. [%cal Rb3a5] } 10. Nxa5 { Your queen is gone for nothing. [%csl Ra5] }) (9... Qc7 { Queen to c7 is the usual Kan square, but from there she no longer guards your knight. } 10. Qxf6 { A knight and a pawn down: with your queen on c7, nothing guarded f6. [%csl Rf6] }) 10. Kf1 { His king steps to f1, as most players do, though e2 was more careful. It looks as if your bishop must now run. Must it? [%csl Rf2] } 10... d6 { Right: the bishop can stay. Your pawn guards e5, so his e pawn cannot chase your knight away. [%cal Gd6e5] } (10... e5 { Just as good: your pawn goes to e5 itself, so his e pawn cannot move at all. [%csl Ge5] } 11. g3 { Better for you: your bishop still sits deep in his camp, and his king is stuck on f1. [%csl Gf2] }) (10... Nc6 { Just as good: the queen knight comes out before any pawn moves. } 11. Nc3 { Slightly better for you: both your knights are out, and his king still has not touched your bishop. [%csl Gf2] }) (10... Rg6 { The rook chases her again, but that sends her off h6. } 11. Qh3 e5 12. Kxf2 { Level, and your edge is gone: with his queen off h6 his king took your bishop, and only his open king makes up for it. [%csl Rf2] }) (10... Ba7 { The bishop runs home, and his e pawn is free to move. } 11. e5 Ng4 12. Qxh7 Rf8 13. Ke2 Nxe5 { About level: you won the pawn back, but his queen got into h7 and your edge is gone. [%csl Rh7] }) (10... Bb6 { Stepping back to b6 lets his e pawn jump at once. } 11. e5 Ng4 12. Qxh7 Kf8 { About level: your knight was kicked to g4, and his queen took h7 with a hit on your rook. [%cal Rh7g8] }) (10... Ng4 { Hitting his queen with the knight lets her grab h7. } 11. Qxh7 Nf6 12. Qh3 Ba7 { About level: his queen took a pawn and got out, and your bishop had to run after all. [%csl Rh3] }) (10... d5 { Striking in the middle lets his e pawn kick your knight. } 11. e5 Ng4 12. Qxh7 Kd7 13. h3 Nxe5 14. Kxf2 { Better for him: his pawn kicked your knight, his queen left h6 for h7, and then his king took your bishop. A piece down. [%csl Rf2] }) (10... Qb6 { The queen guards your bishop, but she was also the knight's only guard. } 11. Qxf6 Bc5 { A knight down: his queen took it, and your bishop had to find a new home. [%csl Rf6] }) (10... Bc5 { Look again: his knight on b3 hits c5. } 11. Nxc5 { A bishop gone for nothing. [%csl Rc5] }) (10... Qc7 { The Kan queen move, but here it takes the only guard off your knight. } 11. Qxf6 Ba7 { A knight down: queen to c7 left f6 bare, and your bishop still had to retreat. [%csl Rf6] }) 11. Bg5 { His bishop comes to g5, and that is a mistake: pawn to g3 was the careful move. Now your knight is attacked twice and guarded once. [%cal Rg5f6,Rh6f6] } 11... Rg6 { Right: your rook hits his queen first, and from g6 it guards your knight too. Now watch. [%cal Gg6h6,Gg6f6][%tail] } (11... Nbd7 { Good too: the knight guards f6 a second time. [%cal Gd7f6] } 12. g3 { Slightly better for you: your knight is safe, but the rook move gained more. [%csl Gd7] }) (11... Ng4 { Moving the knight opens his bishop's line to your queen. } 12. Bxd8 Nxh6 13. Bc7 Ba7 14. Bxd6 { Level: the queens came off, and his bishop picked up a pawn on the way out. [%csl Rd6] }) (11... Rxg5 { Your rook takes the bishop, but his queen takes back. } 12. Qxg5 Ba7 { Better for him: you gave a rook for a bishop, and your own bishop still had to run. [%csl Rg5] }) (11... Ba7 { Saving the bishop first leaves your knight to his bishop. } 12. Bxf6 Qb6 13. Bd4 Qd8 14. Bxa7 Rxa7 { A knight down: his bishop took it, then traded itself for yours. [%csl Rf6] }) (11... Nc6 { Bringing out the other knight leaves this one to his bishop. } 12. Bxf6 Be3 13. Qh4 Qc7 14. Qxh7 Kf8 { A knight and a pawn down: his bishop took the knight, and his queen went on to h7. [%csl Rf6] }) (11... Qc7 { Queen to c7 is a Kan habit, but here she stops guarding your knight. } 12. Qxf6 Ba7 { A knight down: his queen swallowed it, and your bishop went back to a7. [%csl Rf6] }) (11... Qe7 { From e7 the queen still guards the knight, but it is still only one guard. } 12. Bxf6 Be3 13. Bxe7 Bxh6 14. Bxd6 { A knight and a pawn down: the queens came off, and his bishops collected your knight and a pawn. [%csl Rd6] }) 12. Qh5 Ba7 { Nothing of yours hangs, the pawns are level, and his king on f1 can never castle. That is what g7 cost him: better for you. [%csl Ga7] } 0-1


[Event "Sicilian Defence: answer the king on f2"]
