{ Your bishop has taken f2 with check, and his queen stands on h6. }
10. Kxf2 { His king takes your bishop, and that is the losing choice: stepping to e2 held. You are a bishop down. For how long? } 10... Ng4+ { Right: the knight checks his king and hits his queen on h6 at the same time. That is a fork. [%cal Gg4f2,Gg4h6] } (10... Nxe4+ { A check and a pawn, but look at his bishop on d3. } 11. Bxe4 { Much better for him: his bishop took your knight, and now you are a knight and a bishop down for a pawn. [%csl Re4] }) (10... Qb6+ { A check, but it wins nothing back. } 11. Ke1 Ng4 12. Qf4 { Much better for him: his king stepped back, and his queen got away from your knight. You are a bishop down. [%csl Rf4] }) (10... Rxg2+ { Look again: his king guards g2. [%cal Rf2g2] } 11. Kxg2 { Much better for him: his king took your rook as well. [%csl Rg2] }) (10... Rg6 { Your rook chases her, but she simply steps back. } 11. Qd2 { Much better for him: his queen is safe on d2, and you are a bishop down. [%csl Rd2] }) (10... Nc6 { A calm developing move, but a bishop down you need more than that. } 11. Ke1 { Much better for him: his king walked back to safety, and your bishop is gone. [%csl Re1] }) (10... d5 { The pawn strikes in the middle, but your bishop is already gone. } 11. Ke1 { Much better for him: his king is back on e1, and you are simply a piece down. [%csl Re1] }) (10... e5 { Too slow: his king is exposed right now, and the chance will not last. } 11. h3 { Much better for him: his h pawn now covers g4, so your knight can never check there. [%csl Rg4] }) 11. Ke2 { His king steps to e2, and his queen is still attacked. } 11... Nxh6 { Right: your knight takes his queen. Now watch. [%tail] } 12. Bxh6 Nc6 { His bishop took back on h6, and nothing of yours hangs. Count it: his queen is gone for your knight and bishop. That fork is why g7 was bait. [%csl Gg7] } 0-1
